easywall (2.23.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Breaking: the blocklist's kernel log prefix is "easywall blocklist: ".
    Anything that greps the kernel log or packets.log for the old prefix
    finds nothing new.
  * Breaking: 2.22 reads a rules.json written by 2.23 as an empty blocklist
    and allowlist. Export the rules before upgrading if you might go back.
  * New: eight curated feeds and up to three own feeds by URL, each
    switched on individually, all off by default, checked after the
    allowlist. Only the web process downloads; the core checks every list
    it is sent and keeps the last good copy.
  * New: UPDATE_FEED and GET_FEEDS on the socket; /apply names a feed that
    holds your own address; /blocked names the feed that dropped a packet;
    log_feed_connections and its limit.
  * Change: the two lists are called blocklist and allowlist everywhere.
    Old routes redirect, old TOML keys are still read with a warning, and
    rules.json and exports are read in either spelling.
  * Change: an apply is one kernel transaction; a refused apply leaves the
    old rules live instead of an empty table.
  * Change: the socket limit is 8 MiB each way, from 1 MiB.
  * Change: an apply or resume can be refused for a moment while a feed
    refresh holds the apply slot; try again.
  * Fix: the guard against tracked personal addresses was red from the
    release's first commit; the research notes no longer quote one.

