easywall (2.15.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Feat: every port rule carries a kernel packet counter and a stable id, so
    the port pages say when each port last carried a packet the firewall
    accepted, and the dashboard says how many open ports nobody has used in
    thirty days. An open port nobody uses is the most common avoidable
    exposure on a hobby server, and until now nothing here could point at one.
    The figure survives an apply, which rebuilds the table and resets every
    counter in it: the counters are read immediately before the flush and
    accumulated in /var/lib/easywall/usage.json, and the baseline is kept
    there too so a daemon restart does not re-count what it already booked.
    [usage] interval sets how often they are read, 300 seconds by default;
    0 stops the periodic read, not the counting.
  * Fix: ten entries carried forward from earlier releases — two races, four
    paths through apply that failed without writing an audit entry, two guards
    that read less than they claimed, and four wording defects. One more,
    shipped in 2.14 and found here: a request arriving as the daemon shut down
    could be abandoned rather than answered.

