easywall (2.13.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Feat: trusted_proxies, and EASYWALL_WEB_TRUSTED_PROXIES beside it. A list
    of addresses and networks whose X-Forwarded-For header easywall believes.
    When the TCP peer is on that list — and only then — the resolved client
    becomes the address in the audit log, the address the lockout verdict is
    computed for, and the key the login limiter counts against. Empty by
    default, and an empty list behaves exactly as 2.12 did. A list and never a
    boolean: "trust the header" with no way to say whose is
    GHSA-3fxj-6jh8-hvhx, GHSA-rjr7-jggh-pgcp and GHSA-9g5q-2w5x-hmxf, and no
    configuration of easywall can express it. Listing a network wider than the
    proxies it holds hands address spoofing to anything that can reach the
    port from inside it
  * Change: the login limiter now counts per resolved client. Behind a reverse
    proxy it counted per proxy, which is one budget for everybody: five
    attempts per ten minutes shared by every operator, exhausted by one
    attacker. THIS CHANGES BEHAVIOUR FOR ANYONE ALREADY RUNNING BEHIND A
    PROXY — and only once trusted_proxies names that proxy. Until it does,
    nothing changes
  * Change: the via-proxy marker now means the recorded address is a stand-in,
    not a confirmed client. True whenever resolution falls back to the peer —
    an untrusted peer sending a forwarding header, same as before, or a
    trusted one whose header named nobody — and false only when the walk
    actually named a client
  * Change: the demo records no login addresses. Its audit log shows that
    somebody signed in and not from where — the field is omitted, not filled
    with a placeholder
  * Feat: five proofs against a real peer address. The resolution is measured
    over a veth pair, where the peer comes from the kernel rather than from a
    struct a test filled in, and each was watched failing with the feature
    reverted before it was counted as written. The integration job now covers
    internal/web as well as internal/core, with a guard test that fails if
    that path ever narrows again

 -- easywall maintainers <12394156+jp1337@users.noreply.github.com>  Fri, 28 Aug 2026 17:05:36 +0200

easywall (2.12.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Change: a stored value now beats the environment variable naming the same
    key. Until 2.12 the environment overlaid the parsed file, so a value set
    in the interface — or written into web.toml by hand — came back changed
    after a restart, with nothing anywhere saying why. The order is now
    stored value, then environment variable, then built-in default, and it is
    the same order for all thirteen variables. THIS IS A BEHAVIOUR CHANGE FOR
    EXISTING DOCKER INSTALLATIONS: a variable that used to win now loses to a
    value stored in the file. The reset control on /system is the way back
  * Change: "stored" means the file's value differs from the built-in default,
    never that the key is present in the file. -write-config emits every
    default and that file is what the container image ships; the other
    reading would have disabled every environment variable in the product on
    exactly the installations that use them
  * Change: config/web.toml no longer pre-answers the counting question. The
    telemetry = false line is now a commented example, because absent means
    nobody has answered yet — a different state from "no", and under the new
    precedence a pre-answered default would have let EASYWALL_WEB_TELEMETRY
    overrule an operator who genuinely declined
  * Change: the Options, Network and System pages save when you press Save.
    They posted over HTMX on every change *and* carried a Save button, so the
    button reported a write that had already happened. The button stays; the
    automatic write goes
  * Feat: every setting whose key an environment variable names says so on the
    page. The control keeps working; beneath it the interface says either
    "set by the environment" or "the environment says X — overridden here",
    and in the second case offers "Reset to the environment value". Reset
    removes the stored line rather than writing the default, because for a
    flag whose absence is a state those are different files
  * Feat: EASYWALL_WEB_TELEMETRY switches the installation count on from the
    environment, so the public demo reports like any other installation —
    which also means the number means installations including the demo.
    Consent semantics are unchanged: unset still means no, and an answer
    given in the interface beats the variable
  * Feat: a precedence section and a diagram on the Environment Variables
    documentation page, with a Control column saying which variables the
    interface also offers
  * Fix: the counting toggle on the System page can be saved without
    JavaScript. Its form carried no submit button at all — hx-trigger was the
    only thing that ever submitted it — so a script-free operator could move
    a consent switch and never store the answer

 -- easywall maintainers <12394156+jp1337@users.noreply.github.com>  Fri, 28 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000

easywall (2.11.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Feat: a port rule can name who may reach it. Sources is a comma-separated
    list of addresses and networks on every TCP and UDP rule; empty still
    means everyone. Each usable entry becomes its own nft rule matched before
    the port is tested, and a source list holding nothing usable opens the
    port to nobody rather than to the world. /apply gained a matching
    reachability reason, port_source_mismatch
  * Feat: a catalogue of 29 services fills in a rule in one click. "Add from
    catalogue" on /ports appends the ports a service listens on with a
    suggested source restriction already filled in, everything left editable
    and nothing applied until you apply it. The service name rides along as a
    label only
  * Feat: a new aside on /ports explains what an empty Sources field means and
    why the catalogue's private-networks suggestion may not be enough on its
    own over IPv6
  * Fix: the SSH brute-force chain no longer outranks the blacklist. It
    jumped to sshbrute before the blacklist was consulted and ended in
    accept, so a blacklisted address could still open an SSH connection under
    the rate limit. The chain now returns, so traffic under the rate limit
    falls back into the input chain and meets the blacklist, then the
    whitelist, then the port rule, exactly as if the module were not there
  * Change: a host with SSH brute-force protection on and no rule opening
    port 22 no longer has port 22 open by itself; if you were relying on the
    module alone to keep SSH reachable, add an explicit rule for it before
    you apply this release. An under-rate SSH connection now also traverses
    the module rules that follow the jump — connection limit, RST flood,
    broadcast/multicast/anycast drops — which the old accept short-circuited

